What is Broadcast:
------------------
    - A broadcast means sending one packet to every device in the same LAN (same broadcast domain)
    - instead of sending the packet to one specific device, the sender says:
      (I don't know who has this IP address, Everyone, please listen,
      Whoever owns this IP, reply to me)

      Example:
      --------
      Suppose:
      PC1: 192.168.1.10, MAC: AA-AA-AA-AA-AA-AA-AA
      PC2: 192.168.1.20, MAC: BB-BB-BB-BB-BB-BB-BB
      PC3: 192.168.1.30, MAC: CC-CC-CC-CC-CC-CC-CC

      All are connected to the same switch,
      Now PC1 wants to send data to 192.168.1.20

      Step1: Check ARP Cache
            PC1 checks its ARP table,
            is there any entry for 192.168.1.20?
            No.

      Step2: Send ARP Request(Broadcast)
            PC1 creates an ARP request:
            Who has IP address 192.168.1.20? Tell 192.168.1.10.
            The Ethernet destination MAC is:
            FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF: This is called the broadcast MAC address

      Step3: Switch Receives It:
            The switch sees the destination MAC is FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF
            It Understands: This is a broadcast frame

      So the switch copies the frame and sends it out of every port,
      Except the one it came in on.

      That Means:
      -----------
      PC2 receives it, PC3 receives it, Any other devices receive it

      Step4: Only the Correct Device Replies:
      PC2 checks: My IP is 192.168.1.20. This request is for me.

      So PC2 sends an ARP replay (this is unicast, no broadcast):
      192.168.1.20 is at BB-BB-BB-BB-BB-BB
      PC1 stores this mapping in its ARP cache and can now send data directly to PC2.
      



















